{"kind":"request","text":"OAuth on the hosted MCP endpoint (plan 031 item 8, stage 2): design head, then implementation.\n\nAsk: write the design note for OAuth on the hosted MCP endpoint, get it reviewed, then commission its implementation to the builder. Plan 031 item 8 named the steps (identify, validate the client and redirect, consent, audience-bound credential, current authority at each call, revocation) and owed them to stage 2, hosted (notes/plans/031-2026-10-12-generic-mcp-service.md:69). The hosted endpoint landed without them (notes/2026-10-13-hosted-mcp-endpoint.md:33; request 9b3084d4). Today the endpoint takes `Authorization: Bearer \u003cagent\u003e.\u003csecret\u003e` for an agent that an admin enrolled by the three-step controller's recipe (docs/mcp.md, \"Creating an agent: the controller's recipe\").\n\nWhy now (read on 2026-10-20):\n\n1. Every consumer AI app reaches a remote MCP server through OAuth. Meta's Muse connector platform (open since 2026-09-18) takes an \"Existing MCP\" hosted endpoint with auth as API key, OAuth with PKCE, or other; Muse custom connectors are reported to expect a remote streamable-HTTP server with OAuth; Claude's and ChatGPT's connector settings have no header field. Plan 031 recorded this as row 7, \"Hosts where a person cannot set a header (Claude Desktop and web, ChatGPT) could not connect. Unchanged. OAuth: Adopted for stage 2, not built\" (notes/2026-10-01-mcp-plan.md:161). A directory connector is one that any user of that app adds for themselves; a per-agent bearer minted by an admin cannot be that.\n\n2. The sales applications note (landed 3ed2a08a) and the brokered-market pattern discussed today (a roster room where providers enroll from their own AI app, and one engagement room per need) need an outside person to reach a room from the app they are already in, in one step, as themselves: \"an outside party's word is either their own signed act as a member, or a member's signed report of what they said\". With bearer-only enrollment, an admin runs the controller's recipe for each person's agent and hands them a secret out of band. That is not one step, and it does not scale to a roster of providers.\n\n3. The MVP plan lists \"OAuth for remote multi-user access: missing; host\" (notes/2026-10-16-repo-in-ai-mvp-plan.md:204).\n\nWhat the design must settle (questions, not answers):\n\n- Which member signs. A token identifies a person; an act needs a key. Compare: consent performs the controller's recipe and enrolls an agent key under the person's control (the person as recorded controller, traced as entry.ts:56 and grant.ts:139-142 do today), or the Worker holds a person key. Say what the record shows either way.\n- Identify: who runs the sign-in. The Worker has no accounts and dap has no personal account across records (docs/roadmap.md). Compare the room's own invitation as the identity (an `artroom-invite:` link consumed at consent), a membership-held identity, and an external identity provider.\n- The authorization profile: OAuth 2.1 with PKCE, Protected Resource Metadata (RFC 9728), audience-bound tokens (RFC 8707), client ID metadata documents, dynamic client registration deprecated (notes/2026-10-01-mcp-plan.md:259-261). Clients such as Muse publish no client metadata of their own, so the server must accept a client whose metadata is a URL. `@cloudflare/workers-oauth-provider` 1.2.1 implements the 2026-07-28 profile, and \"validating a token gives no Artroom role and no owner-control policy\" (ibid:269-273): the membership read now is the authority at each call, as the controller's recipe already does at its step 3.\n- Lifetime, refresh and revocation: token lifetime; refresh tokens (one community report says Muse custom connectors drop after about an hour when no refresh token is issued); retirement ending every session, as `DELETE /mcp/agents/\u003cagent\u003e` does today.\n- Consent and scope: whether consent names a toolset (builder, reviewer, observer, all exist) or a role, and what it shows the person. The scope still judges each act; consent grants nothing in the record.\n- Where it runs: the scope Worker and rehearsal, as routes beside `POST /mcp`; the local `dap mcp` loopback server unchanged.\n\nAcceptance: a person holding an invitation adds a room as a connector in a named consumer AI app (Claude.ai web or desktop, and one of Muse or ChatGPT) from that app's connector settings, consents, and takes an act that the record shows as their own; a second person cannot use the first's token; retiring the member ends their sessions; the run is recorded per plan 031 section 13 with the client version and configuration.\n\nOrder: the planner places it. It gates the sales applications' outside-party seats (slice C4) and any directory connector submission.","body":{"conditions":"Design note first (notes/, dated); checker review before landing; implementation requests to builder after the note lands; no Worker deployment without review.","stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9b3084d47d342ea1606a38518db159a4eb7f6cf8 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:03f7c6dc8bca81f88ae1f3a5c8e2212a745c7220, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0757612c14a8e52aecede892651c4d5c49e6e906, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:0ac4011373193a7627f4061c127d23e7550fe64e, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1003371cc5e78065faeac7e4b6b609648c0587cf and 58 more","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}